$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):EXP_PROC
$DATUM General Methods for Glycopeptide Synthesis. Glycopeptides derived from
the GAIIQTPTLG sequence were synthesized by an automatic solid-phase
peptide synthesizer (Applied Biosystems, ABI 433A) on Rink amide resin
(79 mg, 0.05 mmol, loading: 0.63 mmol/g) or Sieber amide resin (70.4 mg,
0.05 mmol, loading: 0.71 mmol/g) via Nα-Fmoc-based approach.
2-(1H-Benzotriazole-1-yl)-oxy-1,1,3,3-tetramethyl-uronium
hexafluorophosphate (HBTU)/1-hydroxybenzotriazole (HOBt) were employed as
the coupling reagents. The coupling of glycosylated amino acids was
performed manually using
2-(7-aza-1H-benzotriazole-1-yl)-1,1,3,3-tetramethyluronium
hexafluorophosphate (HATU)/N,N-diisopropylethylamine (DIPEA) as the
activating reagents. Upon completion of the manual coupling reaction as
determined by the Kaiser test, resins were resubjected to the automatic
synthesizer and completed the elongation.
Ac-Gly-Ala-Ile-Ile-Gln-Thr[Man(6-PO4)-α-O]-Pro-Thr-Leu-Gly-NH2 (29).
According to general method for glycopeptide synthesis, compound 29 was
prepared using Sieber amide resin (70.4 mg, 0.05 mmol, loading: 0.71
mmol/g). The first four amino acids were coupled using the peptide
synthesizer. The glycosylated monosaccharide threonine (S4) was coupled
manually. Thus, to a solution of compound S4 (85 mg, 0.1 mmol) in DMF (2
mL) was added HATU (38 mg, 0.1 mmol) and N,N-diisopropylethylamine
(DIPEA) (35 μL, 0.2 mmol). After premixing for 1 min, the pre-activated
amino acid solution was added to compound S5 and agitated for 18 h to
give compound S6. Upon completion of the coupling reaction, as determined
by the Kaiser test,2 compound S6 was resubjected to automated peptide
chain elongation to give the resin-bound glycopeptide S7. Compound S7 was
treated with 1% TFA/DCM (2 mL) for 5 min and the organic solution was
filtered into 5% pyridine in MeOH (4 mL). The cleavage procedure was
repeated 5 times and the combined fractions were concentrated to ~ 5 mL.
The glycopeptide was precipitated by the addition of cold (0 °C) diethyl
ether (40 mL) and retrieved by centrifuging (20 min at 3000 rpm). The
crude product was purified by size-exclusion chromatography (LH-20,
DCM/MeOH, 1/1, v/v) to give glycopeptide S7 (non-resin bound) as an
amorphous white solid. Glycopeptide S7 was treated with TFA/TIPS/DCM
(5/5/90) for 1h and the resulting mixture was concentrated to dryness
under reduced pressure to afford an amorphous white solid. The crude
product was then dissolved in MeOH/DCM (4 mL, 1/1, v/v) and Pd/C (70 mg)
was added and the resulting mixture was placed under a hydrogen
atmosphere (1 psi). After stirring for 24 h, the catalyst was filtered
off and washed thoroughly with MeOH and DCM. The combined filtrates (~50
mL) were concentrated under reduced pressure to afford an amorphous white
solid which was treated with 5% anhydrous hydrazine in MeOH (3 mL) for 1
h (monitored by MALDI-TOF MS). The crude product was purified by reversed
phase HPLC on an analytical C-18 column using a gradient of 0→100%
acetonitrile in H2O (0.1% TFA) over a 50 min-period to give, after
lyophilization of the appropriate fractions. Glycopeptide 29 (9.4 mg,
15%, base on initial loading of the resin) as an amorphous white solid:
HR MALDI-TOF MS: m/z: calcd for C51H89N12O22P [M+Na]+: 1275.5850; found:
1275.5880.
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):NOTES
$DATUM low pressure (in stage 12), solid-supported reaction, peptide synthesizer
used in stages 1-4 and 6-8, attachment to Sieber amide resin in stage 1,
amino acid preactivated and used in stage 5, reactant assumed in stage 9
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):TITLE
$DATUM Synthetic, Structural, and Biosynthetic Studies of an Unusual
Phospho-Glycopeptide Derived from α-Dystroglycan
